Subject: [PATCH] fix: never give JWT_SECRET to external verifiers; retain
 sessions on failed broker teardown (PR #173 fifth review round)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two High-severity findings from the fifth Copilot review round:

- docs/gates/upcloud-worker-factory.md's Gate 1 text told a future
  external (UpCloud-side) verifier to "compute
  deriveWorkerSessionKey(JWT_SECRET) itself" — which means handing that
  verifier the hub's root JWT_SECRET so it can derive the worker key
  locally. That defeats the entire point of key separation: an external
  component holding JWT_SECRET is one compromise away from an attacker
  minting admin-api and runtime tokens too, not just worker-session ones.
  No code in this repo ever transmits JWT_SECRET externally (confirmed by
  inspection — purely a documentation-contract defect), but corrected in
  place before any UpCloud-side build relies on the bad guidance: an
  external verifier is provisioned only the already-derived key value,
  out-of-band, never JWT_SECRET itself. Noted asymmetric signing
  (distributing only a public key) as the stronger design to adopt for
  the real external verifier.

- endSession() swallowed a failed broker-side session-teardown call and
  deleted the session from local state regardless. Credentials were
  (correctly) still revoked unconditionally, but the remote worker and its
  still-valid session token could remain live with no record left to retry
  tearing it down. WorkerSessionState gained an optional teardownPending
  flag; on a broker-teardown failure the session is now retained (with its
  credential scopes cleared, since those are already revoked) instead of
  discarded, and a new listPendingTeardownSessions() surfaces it. Calling
  endSession() again for the same sessionId retries only the broker-side
  DELETE.

